# 5-Minute Quickstart

**ContextUnity ACDD** is a single local Rust binary (zero background daemons, zero cloud dependencies) that upgrades any AI coding agent (Claude Code, Codex, Antigravity, Cursor) or CI script with three capabilities:

- **Code & Docs Graph (`.acdd/code-map.sqlite`)**: Tree-sitter AST symbol reads, caller/callee blast radius, test discovery, and BM25 documentation search.
- **Architectural Linter (`acdd lint`)**: Machine-enforced crate/folder boundaries (`acdd.yaml`), executable Markdown `INV-*` rules, duplicate-code detection, and dead-code checks.
- **Gate State Machine (`.acdd/state.sqlite`)**: Step-by-step verified workflows (`build` → `independent review` → `deliver` → `handoff`) across isolated Git worktrees.

You can adopt ACDD incrementally — start with AST navigation, companion skills, and linting on day one, then adopt verified gates whenever you want stricter engineering guarantees.

---

## Step 1: Install the binary & companion skills

Run the one-line installer on Linux or macOS:

```bash
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ContextUnity/contextunity-acdd/main/install.sh | sh
```

What `install.sh` sets up automatically:

- **`~/.local/bin/acdd`**: SHA-256 verified single binary (`MCP server` + `CLI` in one executable).
- **Companion Agent Skills (`~/.agents/skills/`)**: 5 `SKILL.md` playbooks automatically symlinked into your detected coding agents (`~/.claude/skills`, `~/.codex/skills`, `~/.gemini/config/skills`, `~/.cursor/skills`).
- **`.mcp.json` registration**: wires `"contextunity-acdd": { "command": "acdd", "args": ["serve"] }` in your current project directory.

> **Built from source (`cargo install --path . --locked`)?** Run `./skills/install.sh` once from the cloned repo to install and symlink the 5 companion skills.

Confirm `~/.local/bin` is on your `PATH`:

```bash
acdd --version
```

### Why companion skills matter

Connecting MCP gives your agent **tools**, while **companion skills** teach your agent **how and when** to use them instead of falling back to blind `grep` or `cat` loops:

| Bundled Skill | When the Agent Loads It | What It Teaches the Agent |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **`acdd`** | Code exploration, symbol lookup, or gate execution | Prefer `acdd_read(selector=...)` and `code_map_*` over `grep`/`cat`, plus self-claim (`acdd_claim` → `acdd_verify` → `acdd_submit`) and blackboard handoffs. |
| **`contract-planning`** | Planning features, milestones, or `blueprint_synthesis` | Audit canonical owners, reconcile ADRs/`INV-*` invariants, and split work into small tasks with disjoint write scopes. |
| **`test-suite-rules`** | Writing, modifying, or reviewing tests | Test through real production seams, use table-driven matrices, and avoid temp-file or state leaks. |
| **`close-review-findings`** | Fixing bugs or handling `review` rejections | Trace root causes across all entrypoints (CLI, MCP, internal handlers) so fixes do not patch only one surface. |
| **`agent-instructions`** | Updating `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`, ADRs, or docs | Keep agent routers concise and structure Markdown (`doc_type`, headings, symbol links, `INV-*`) for ACDD indexing. |

---

## Step 2: Initialize your repository

From the root of any Git project, generate starter configuration files:

```bash
acdd init
```

`acdd init` creates three things:

- **`acdd.yaml`**: workspace config declaring `preset: full`, indexed `roots:`, `ignore:` exclusions, `workflows_dirs: [.acdd/workflows]`, and named `specs:` configurations (`milestones` and `plans` by default, backed by `crates/acdd-spec`).
- **`.acdd/workflows/default.yaml`**: workspace gate workflow inheriting the built-in `code` lifecycle (where you bind your project's test command).
- **`.gitignore` guard**: ignores `.acdd/code-map.sqlite` and `.acdd/state.sqlite` while keeping `.acdd/workflows/` and `.acdd/specs/` tracked in Git.

Starter `acdd.yaml` created by `acdd init`:

```yaml
preset: full
roots:
  - .
ignore:
  - target
  - node_modules
  - .venv
  - .git
specs:
  milestones:
    path: docs/milestones
    prefixes: [AC]
    digits: 3
  plans:
    path: docs/plans
    prefixes: [PLAN]
    digits: 3
workflows_dirs:
  - .acdd/workflows
state_db: .acdd/state.sqlite
```

- **`preset`**: selects active subsystems (`full`, `index`, `spec`, `state`, `minimal`); `spec` enables `validate`/`roadmap`/`draft` and the 4 docs/core MCP tools with zero `.acdd/state.sqlite` creation.
- **`roots`**: folders ACDD indexes for AST symbols, call edges, and Markdown docs.
- **`specs`**: user-defined specification configurations (used later by `acdd draft` — you can rename `milestones`/`plans` or add entries like `features` or `rfcs`).

Build the initial code and documentation graph:

```bash
acdd build .
```

---

## Step 3: Explore your codebase & run the linter

Try querying symbols and checking architectural health from your terminal:

```bash
# 1. High-level summary of languages, entrypoints, and docs
acdd query overview

# 2. Search symbols by prefix, name, or parameter/return type
acdd query search "parse*" --kind function

# 3. Read an exact symbol body in one shot (verified by SHA-256 digest)
acdd read src/main.rs --selector main

# 4. Compute blast radius of staged Git changes
acdd query impact --changes staged --depth 1

# 5. Run architectural, syntax, clone, and dead-code lint
acdd lint --categories all

# 6. Read built-in CLI guidance (or pass --mcp for MCP tool guidance)
acdd guide code
```

---

## Step 4: Connect your AI coding agent (MCP + Skills)

`install.sh` automatically updates `.mcp.json` in the folder where you ran it. For any other repository or MCP client (Claude Code, Codex, Antigravity, Cursor), ensure `acdd serve` is registered:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "contextunity-acdd": {
      "command": "acdd",
      "args": ["serve"]
    }
  }
}
```

How the two pieces work together inside your agent session:

- **MCP Server (`acdd serve`)**: exposes 18 built-in tools and auto-refreshes `.acdd/code-map.sqlite` whenever files change.
- **Companion Skills (`~/.agents/skills/`)**: guide your agent to call `code_map_search`, `acdd_read`, `code_map_impact`, and `code_map_lint` automatically instead of reading whole files line-by-line.

---

## Step 5: Run your first verified workflow

Bind your project's test command in `.acdd/workflows/default.yaml`:

```yaml
version: 2
inherit: code

commands:
  build_check:
    command: "cargo test {test_filter}"     # or "pytest -q {test_filter}", "npm test"
    test_filter: "{test}"
  verify_all: "cargo test --workspace"
```

You have two ways to start a verified run — **Option A (`--goal`)** for everyday tasks without extra files, or **Option B (Spec file)** for larger multi-task features.

### Option A: Everyday bugfixes & focused changes (`--goal`, no spec file)

Pass `--goal` to create an ephemeral single-task run (`goal.build` → `goal.review` → `goal.deliver`). Under `inherit: code`, `acdd next` starts at `blueprint_synthesis.synthesize` before `goal.build` — or jumps straight to `goal.build` if you add a lightweight `hotfix` overlay in `.acdd/workflows/hotfix.yaml` (see [Overriding Workflows](/docs/workflows/overriding-workflows/)):

```bash
# Start an isolated run scoped to src/auth/ and tests/
acdd start --goal "Reject expired JWTs before DB lookup" --scope src/auth/ --scope tests/

# Inspect the next ready cursor (`blueprint_synthesis.synthesize`, or `goal.build` with --workflow hotfix)
acdd next

# Claim the ready gate (prints the 6-tier Markdown contract & code context; target worktrees with -C)
acdd claim

# Once inside goal.build, run verification + lint checks (or use `acdd exec -- cargo test`) and advance
acdd verify goal.build --test rejects_expired_jwt
acdd submit goal.build --action pass
```

### Option B: Multi-task specifications (`milestones` / `plans`)

When a feature is too big for one agent turn and needs multiple coordinated tasks, use a **Specification** (`SpecDocument`):

- **What is a Spec (Milestone or Plan)?**: A Git-tracked Markdown contract declaring acceptance criteria (`AC-*`), governing invariants (`INV-*`), and a DAG of `### task: <id>` blocks with non-overlapping file `scope:` globs so multiple agents can build in parallel waves.
- **Where does `milestones` come from?**: `milestones` is **not** a magic built-in keyword — it is simply the spec configuration name defined under `specs:` in `acdd.yaml` during `acdd init` (`milestones` → `docs/milestones/AC-001-...md`, `plans` → `docs/plans/PLAN-001-...md`). You can rename or add any key under `specs:` in `acdd.yaml`.
- **Who writes the spec?**: Either scaffold the next numbered file with `acdd draft <spec> <slug>` and fill in the tasks (manually or with your agent), **or** copy `skills/acdd/references/acdd-workflow-examples/spec_planning.yaml` to `.acdd/workflows/spec_planning.yaml` and run `acdd start --goal "Plan JWT rotation" --workflow spec_planning` to have an architect agent synthesize and review the spec first.

Scaffold and launch a spec from your `milestones` configuration:

```bash
# 1. Allocate the next free ID in specs.milestones -> docs/milestones/AC-001-jwt-rotation.md
acdd draft milestones jwt-rotation --title "JWT Key Rotation"

# 2. After filling in AC-* criteria and ### task: blocks, start the run in an isolated Git worktree
acdd start docs/milestones/AC-001-jwt-rotation.md --worktree

# 3. Target the worktree uniformly via -C (or ACDD_WORKTREE / ACDD_RUN) and run arbitrary commands via `acdd exec`
acdd -C .worktrees/AC-001-jwt-rotation status
acdd exec -- cargo test --workspace
```

What the drafted `docs/milestones/AC-001-jwt-rotation.md` contract looks like:

````markdown
---
id: AC-001-jwt-rotation
title: "JWT Key Rotation"
status: planned
owners: [src/auth/, tests/auth_test.rs]
acceptance_criteria:
  - "AC-1: Tokens signed with the previous active key remain valid during grace window."
---

### task: rotation-verifier
```yaml
task_ref: rotation-verifier
depends_on: []
scope: [src/auth/jwt.rs, tests/auth_test.rs]
```
````

Once started, you can either run gates from the CLI (`acdd next` → `acdd claim` → `acdd verify` → `acdd submit`) or ask your MCP-connected AI orchestrator to drive the run — spawning worker subagents per wave, enforcing independent review (`independent_from: [build]`), and landing atomic verified commits.
